We started this project with one big question: *What is indie rock?* Our answer is these 100 albums that offer one definition of the genre. It is not the only definition, but it is ours, the one we think best captures indie rock’s spirit as it was and continues to be.

A top summary of our definition of indie rock might read something like this: Indie rock is primarily an American musical art form that began in earnest around 1980, started to really take shape in 1988, and has since splintered into many forms. It started as a musical byproduct of punk rock and became a commercial product in pop culture. With rare exceptions, there should be guitars, drums, and a singer. With no exceptions, it should be released on an independent label. (Well, with one exception.) It should speak to and incorporate other genres without fully becoming another genre. It tends to be made by people who are apart (or set themselves apart) from mainstream culture, and therefore tends to be associated with losers and depression, or cool kids and art school. It should, by some estimation, rock.

I could go on and on, but to be too authoritatively prescriptive about what indie rock is flies against its very spirit. Your definition of indie rock is probably just as valid. In 1991, Lou Barlow of Dinosaur Jr. and Sebadoh adroitly defined indie rock in just eight words: “A new generation of electric white boy blues.” It’s why I asked Barlow and over 30 more musicians, label heads, and writers to talk about what indie rock is, and, just as importantly, what indie rock isn’t. It opened up a conversation about semantics, community, radical inclusions, and outright dismissals. I highly encourage you to [read that feature here](https://pitchfork.com/story/what-is-indie-rock-we-asked-33-artists-labels-and-critics) to get a sense of the many different shapes and sizes of indie rock—from the minds of those who’ve lived it.

Let me now introduce you to Larson’s Law of Lists: **The first reaction to any list is to point out what is not on it.** You will scan this list, and your primary impulse will be to think of an album that’s not here. This is good; embrace this. It is human nature. It is especially good for this list, because what is not here is also what shapes our definition of indie rock.

There is no Velvet Underground, no Breeders, no Strokes, no LCD Soundsystem—all of whom put out their major works through major labels. There is no My Bloody Valentine, no Cocteau Twins, no Fall, no Joanna Newsom—all of whom belong primarily to one genre over indie rock. There are countless other albums we love that we put through our silly little crucible of “what is indie rock” that failed to speak to what was happening elsewhere on the list. What’s great is that there is another Best Indie Rock Albums of All Time list that doesn’t care about major labels or genre priority or American exceptionalism and I sincerely encourage you to make it.

One more aspect of our definition: All of these albums share something in common with at least one other album on this list. This is why we asked the artist [Marco Quadri](https://www.instagram.com/marcoquasi/?hl=en) to illustrate 85 different categories that further organize the sound, region, influence, style, drug habits, vibe, and broader cultural impact of the albums on this list. We offer them in good fun, but we also hope they show—in their own cheeky way—the common threads that weave through the history of indie rock.

Modern commerce demands a ranked list, and we stand by the fact that some albums weigh heavier in the balance of music history. But ultimately, this is a celebration of albums that all share a common language while speaking in a vast range of dialects. The means of its production, its sonic and lyrical traits, and its emotional signatures have all become connected through an unseen evolutionary process. Whether by natural selection or immaculate conception, indie rock will live forever. This is our attempt to put a highly contested border around a somewhat arbitrary group of bands and labels whose work prides itself on experimentation, ingenuity, and making art exactly how they wanted to. –Jeremy D. Larson

