Before Bill Callahan became Bill Callahan, the wisecracking, heartbreaking, gravel-voiced statesman of American song, he was Smog: a grimly funny unreliable narrator from the murkiest corners of the lo-fi underground. *Knock Knock* marked the first of his many transformations. With a full band and producer Jim O’Rourke, Callahan moved past the claustrophobic 4-track fantasias of his early recordings and folded in Southern rock, children’s choirs, and atmospheric balladry. Channeling what he called a teenager’s energy, he shed the dirgelike air of his previous albums and sounds by turns unburdened, contemplative, and tender, his koan-like lyrics sharper than ever. There are even a few straight-up rock songs, though one look at the lyrics to “Cold-Blooded Old Times” confirms that there’s nothing straightforward about them. A breakup album from a self-proclaimed visitor from outer space, *Knock Knock* is a rare self-portrait from an artist who would spend another two decades outrunning his own autobiography. –Philip Sherburne

