Since at least Hank Williams, certain images have suggested depression to American songwriters: whip-poor-wills, lonesome whistles, empty highways. They’re all on the lyric sheet to Jason Molina’s *Magnolia Electric Co.*, as are direct references to Big Star and Dead Moon, and the album captures the feeling of finding strength in other people’s writing. Maybe that’s why the songs feel so emboldened. Rather than suffer alone, Molina puts himself in league with *On the Beach* -era Neil Young, the Kris Kristofferson of “Sunday Morning Coming Down,” George Jones at his most desperate, and Richard and Linda Thompson at their most resilient. The songs are no happier—just listen to “Almost Was Good Enough”—but they feel open and full of understanding and acceptance, the way you might feel when you hear a song that captures an obscure sadness you’ve struggled to name. Via scions like MJ Lenderman and Squirrel Flower, it’s a gift the late songwriter is still giving us. –Sadie Sartini Garner

