Sub Pop ✦ 2018

It’s fitting, for a slowcore band, for their masterpiece to arrive a quarter-century into their career. Its opening moments are startling: all hiss and crackle, like you had suddenly dropped whatever digital listening device you were using underwater. From there, the record wraps you in a warped embrace, layering corroded textures and grainy noise over the band’s spare, patient compositions.

Made with producer BJ Burton, *Double Negative* captured the dread and unease of the first Trump administration, when everything—the news, social norms, democratic institutions, the concept of mutually acknowledged truth—felt corrupted. Reality was messy and distorted; rather than shy away from that fact, Low spelunked into it, replicated it. Alan Sparhawk and Mimi Parker sing about “the end of hope” on “Dancing and Fire,” their sublime harmonies shining through the din like a prism. But *Double Negative* also contains an inherent optimism: in the band’s appetite for new ideas, even after decades of making music together; in the way Parker and Sparhawk’s intertwined voices still seemed capable of sanctifying any darkness. –Marissa Lorusso

