It’s hard to explain just how wild it was to hear an honest-to-god rock’n’roll sax solo on a major indie rock record. Sure, there may have been some alto folded tastefully into the wind charts on Grizzly Bear albums. But this dude was blowing like Bobby Keys on *Sticky Fingers.* All the honkin’ and struttin’ on “Coronado” said it more clearly than anything else: Deerhunter had reached that magic moment in a lucky band’s career where every choice is the right one, the peak of their audacity coinciding precisely with their audience’s greatest excitement. It’s not that the band had overhauled its approach: *Halcyon Digest* ’s mix of jangle and churn, post-punk scraping and dream-pop sensuality, followed clearly from their previous records. But everything glowed with more confidence than before, both the songs that blew your hair back and those that dropped the veil of noise to reveal a new directness of feeling and melody. Its title’s retrospective tint proved prophetic: Nine months into the new decade, it was the belated final bloom of indie’s late-’00s moment in the sun, the end of an era when a guy like Bradford Cox—strange, antagonistic, vulnerable, pure—could become a real-deal rock star. –Andy Cush

