Doug Martsch understood, maybe before anyone else did, that the magic of indie rock lay somewhere between trying your damndest and accepting, with a shrug and a sigh, that you wouldn’t make it. On *There’s Nothing Wrong With Love*, he strikes a near-mystical balance between these impulses, yielding a masterpiece of half-remembered rock-god visions and tender indie-pop daydreams. Martsch’s scrabbling guitar solo on “Distopian Dream Girl” arches towards the heavens, hitting glaring wrong notes all along its path. “Car” crams more words into its verses than can fit. Martsch reels off immortal lines like “He thought an Albertson’s stir-fry dinner would make his apartment a home” while cheerily insisting they mean nothing, just made-up nonsense to fill space. Later, he’d vow to be “perfect.” For now, he just wanted to show us movies of his dreams. –Jayson Greene

