On rare occasions, a human reaches beyond their capabilities and into the divine. Glaswegian art-rock band Life Without Buildings aren’t religious, but their sole album, *Any Other City*, lands like a spirit-changing epiphany. Freeform instrumentation and forlorn guitar plucks lay the ground for vocalist Sue Tompkins to stutter, pout, and exclaim with unfiltered zeal. Her tales of infatuation and adventure are akin to cut-up poetry assembled from newspaper clippings, diary entries, and signage flashing by a car window. She delivers words like she’s sounding out their spelling in kindergarten—“pl- *eeea* -suh,” “w-w-ways”—inviting you to see the world through the eyes of unburdened youth. That purity in Tompkins’ performance is almost destabilizing on first listen, an intoxicating reminder of how to rewire your brain and escape rigidity—especially when your peers can’t help but take their art form too seriously. “I’m gonna persuade you!” Tompkins chirps at the album’s very beginning. She’s right. –Nina Corcoran

