There’s an old Lucy Sante line about the Mekons that keeps coming to mind: “They built an imaginary America out of pocket lint.” TV on the Radio didn’t have to imagine America, but they built their own anyway: out of street-corner doo-wop harmonies and Larry Graham’s fuzz pedal, gospel benediction and slow-jam sleaze, with synth pads and tremolo guitars smeared overhead like smog in a summer sky. An expansive palette, and yet something of a pocket-lint quality, too: the drum machines suggest beats but don’t commit, as if adding a snare would be too big a battery drain; the harmonies might have been recorded in bedroom closets. It gave the sense of bricolage, of post-apocalyptic resourcefulness—which, for a New York band in the post-9/11 Bush II years, was probably no accident. It sounded expansive and intimate, Biblical and sexy, doomed and ecstatic. I loved how the vocal layers of “Staring at the Sun” just kept piling up, how that hi-hat fooled you into thinking the song was going to open up any moment and offer some sort of release that never came. Tunde Adebimpe wrote songs about everything: God, sex, TV dinners, world wars. Invocations and dirty words, all expressing one thing: “Oh, fucking eternal.” –Andy Cush

