If the words “indie rock” once signified “tossed-off” or “casual,” the Arcade Fire ended that. With *Funeral*, this fundamentally agnostic genre was given its first revivalist preachers. Before Win Butler, Régine Chassagne, Richard Reed Parry, and their assembled merry band stormed the stage—hollering and whooping, scaling rafters, playing every instrument like a drum, including each other’s helmeted heads—an “indie rock band” typically consisted of four people, swaying noncommittally, fixed gazes into the middle distance. Indie rock *songs* focused on small things: parking lots, haircuts, buzzards and dreadful crows. The songs on *Funeral* started with the apocalypse, with visions of orphaned children tunneling out from collapse, swinging from dead powerlines, dancing in abandoned streets. The music moved in huge, sweeping arcs, like ocean liners or a Bruckner symphony. By the time Chassagne let out her wordless howl at the close of “In the Backseat,” the Arcade Fire had kicked open the basement-club doors, inviting everyone to run out screaming underneath the skies. –Jayson Greene

