In 2003, a songwriter named Sufjan Stevens released a record about his home state of Michigan, and later he went on to make two records’ worth— *IIlinois* and its companion *The Avalanche* (2006)—about mine. *Illinois* (or *Come on Feel the Illinoise* for fun) is Stevens’ first masterpiece, a collection of personal snapshots, archival references, and antique instrumental flourishes lovingly restored in a painterly style of his own. It’s part folk opera, part hand-drawn highway map, deliriously overwrought and devastatingly sad. In songs with incredibly long titles and even longer instrumental codas, Stevens revived obscure monuments, bowed to Miles Davis, even made the banjo look sexy. This was conspicuously not a record any major label would make, but it was a cult smash such that a lot of people seemed to sincerely believe that Stevens would eventually produce *48 more* state albums, like minting collectible quarters. They were jealous of *Illinois*! This is a traveling album, a spiritual pilgrimage full of detours and trips to New York and Decatur that culminates, with Gothic apropos, in the city of architecture, at the highest point of the tallest tower. You feel like you can see God. –Anna Gaca

