## Wilco: Yankee Hotel Foxtrot

![Image may contain Baseball Cap Cap Clothing Hat Bottle and Person](https://media.pitchfork.com/photos/6aee2f972e98f66215d0bb0b/master/w_1600,c_limit/Wilco%20%E2%80%94%20Yankee%20Hotel%20Foxtrot.jpg)

Image may contain Baseball Cap Cap Clothing Hat Bottle and Person

![Image may contain City Architecture Building High Rise Urban Condo Housing Skyscraper Apartment Building and Tower](https://media.pitchfork.com/photos/6aee2be6d19586adfe8e3566/master/w_1600,c_limit/Wilco%20%E2%80%93%20Yankee%20Hotel%20Foxtrot.jpg)

Image may contain City Architecture Building High Rise Urban Condo Housing Skyscraper Apartment Building and Tower

When Jeff Tweedy learned in 2001 that Reprise Records didn’t much care for his six-song sampler of Wilco’s unfinished fourth record, there was an illicit thrill: “It made me feel we were doing something right,” he later admitted. He barreled ahead alongside textural wizard Jim O’Rourke, peeling layers of noise and static away from the mix of power-pop anthems and broken ballads his band had fastidiously built during the past year. When Reprise rejected the result as unmarketable, Wilco said they’d rather be dropped than change anything. Reprise accepted their exit, only to have another imprint owned by the same mega-corp sign Wilco after the band shared *Yankee Hotel Foxtrot* for free online to great acclaim.

The debacle helped rewrite the rules of where indie rock ended and so-called corporate rock began, but that was largely because of the way its 11 songs sounded—classic rock tunes shot through with guitars that felt fanged, heartbroken confessionals streaked with dins that felt hallucinatory, heartland numbers undermined by drums and drones that felt vertiginous. It wasn’t that the majors paid twice for any record that made *Yankee Hotel Foxtrot* mythic; it was that they did so for a record that moved like *this*, where ideas unapologetically imported from 20th-century composition and power electronics and free jazz emboldened and enriched the songs. Those textures emphasized the fault lines of Tweedy’s id and ego, making it all anxious and urgent and real, the sound of an unsettled mind trying to find some center. Wilco became indie rock’s Robin Hood and, in turn, one of its most venerable exports. –Grayson Haver Currin

