*Bee Thousand* is a fucked-up cabinet of curiosities. Open this drawer and you’ll find a necklace of 50 eyeballs, a prize from some sort of bear-training contest. Open that one, and there’s a snapshot of a hardcore UFO cresting over Midwestern rooftops. But open a third, and you’ll find the scattered confessions of Robert Pollard: a disillusioned elementary school teacher, a relentless self-critic, a regular guy coming into middle age in middle America. A product of sporadic recording sessions with a rotating cast of players in “the Snakepit,” Pollard’s makeshift home studio, *Bee Thousand* is one of indie rock’s great world-building projects. These songs take place in a Pollard-verse, a Henry Darger world of violent childish wonder populated by scavenger queens and kickable elves. Some of the album’s wonders take the form of abrasive song fragments, seemingly recorded on the proverbial potato. Others have been compressed into gemlike pop constructions of staggering beauty. The very best have elements of both. On “Gold Star for Robot Boy,” it sounds like the band is rushing to get down an idea before it vanishes. It is a gloriously underbaked pop confection. In indie rock, some things just taste better with a gooey center. –Mitch Therieau

