Image may contain Face Head Person Photography Portrait Flower Petal Plant Anemone and Baby

When Chan Marshall was 25 years old, she was gripped by nightmarish hallucinations so severe that she wrote the bulk of *Moon Pix*, her fourth album, just to get her mind off them. Then she called her label Matador and asked, “Would y’all pay for my ticket to Australia so I can get the fuck out of where I’m at?” She made *Moon Pix* there, with post-rock sorcerers Jim White and Mick Turner of the Dirty Three; its spartan, spacious sound glints like the burning embers of that band’s fireworks. Ghosts of Marshall’s Southern roots haunt her—specters of rock and blues, “Amazing Grace” and “shoop-shoopa-doo,” heat and thunder—an alchemy that created a tear in the fabric of music. On the other side was a self-confrontation so stark, and a solitude inhabited so fully, it unleashed a hypnotic indie-rock unconscious as elemental as its own season. The other side is where Lana Del Rey, Waxahatchee, and Sky Ferreira grew up, in *Moon Pix* ’s eternal twilight. “You will be changed and everything,” Marshall incants on “Metal Heart.” “I could stay here, become someone better,” she sings on the prayerful “Colors and the Kids,” a piano ballad and a rite of passage, like squinting straight through the piercing sun of your own future. –Jenn Pelly

