Image may contain François Walthry Architecture Building Factory Adult Person Book Comics and Publication

*Diamond Jubilee* arrived with as little fanfare as possible: a Geocities page, a “Full Album HQ” upload, a dejected avatar perched in front of grain silos. The internet inevitably did the rest. Two years on, that lowkey drop serves only as prelude to its ascent into the hall of all-timers. Patrick Flegel was no stranger to [brilliance](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0aq4ytbDv68), neither as part of Women nor as alter ego Cindy Lee for a decade, accessorizing heartbreak with a pert set of Nancy boots. Here, Cindy is reborn as either the world’s most lonesome individual or its greatest performance artist, hitching up at each three-seat saloon in every two-star town with curlicues of Lucky Strike smoke in her wake.

The sound of *Diamond Jubilee* is a revelation, transposing the swimmy quality of ’90s indie onto the sort of girl-group arrangements largely perfected a half-century ago. With lyrical economy and a finely calibrated melodic ear, Flegel riffs not only on the hearthside crackle of vintage 45s, but also their emotional undertow. When the stereo field in “Wild One” splits open, or the cherubs ride in to drape strings across “24/7 Heaven,” you might find yourself double-checking if these astonishingly self-assured songs weren't timeworn standards all along. Well, they are now. –Gabriel Szatan

