![Image may contain Dominic Savio Art Painting Baby Person Adult Clothing and Hat](https://media.pitchfork.com/photos/6aee2be66de58f9ada4c1c67/master/w_1600,c_limit/Neutral%20Milk%20Hotel%20%E2%80%93%20In%20the%20Aeroplane%20Over%20the%20Sea.jpg)

Image may contain Dominic Savio Art Painting Baby Person Adult Clothing and Hat

Most music belongs to the world. *In the Aeroplane Over the Sea* belongs behind a curtain, through a door, in a back room, inside a jar. Letting this album breathe the same stupid air we breathe now seems inhumane. Delicate music like this conjures thoughts and images so private that there is little more to do with the feeling than sit with it alone, and let the lowest frequencies of longing and shame ring out inside your body. There is no one interpretation as to why Anne Frank haunts this album. There are no screenshots of a well-framed green-fleshy flower or flesh-licking lady to post. Just a compact between you and Jeff Mangum that, if you want, you can give up your world for 40 minutes and go to “a secret place where no one dares to go.”

A few notes before you do: Mangum’s voice is more oboe than not; this album’s mythical standing as a totemic statement of indie rock has created a long-lasting cultural disinfluence campaign that makes some people physically ill when they hear the words “Neutral Milk Hotel”; some might call this “circus music.” I’d call it Henry Darger’s *Sgt. Pepper’s.* How can something so subconsciously bizarre also be such a precise musical statement? Mangum’s words spill out exactly into place, and the motifs (musical notes! spines! semen!) are aligned as if through some divine intervention. “Can’t believe how strange it is to be anything at all” is one of the few lines from the album that has escaped the jar over the years. It’s our species’ biggest wonder, but it’s just one of a thousand on this record. –Jeremy D. Larson

