Image may contain Candle

Before *Daydream Nation,* Sonic Youth were one of SST’s great bands: punk-rock envelope-shredders who invented a whole new vocabulary out of unusual tunings and drumsticks on strings. After it, they were one of the greatest bands that America has produced, the architects of a wall between *before* and *after* within independent rock music that’s still visible from space.

Their final indie release before the major-label *Goo* followed in the footsteps of former labelmates’ *Double Nickels on the Dime* and *Zen Arcade*: a gatefold double LP whose excesses hinted at the limitlessness of the band’s vision. It’s a case study in compression and expansion. Coiled riffs explode into whirling maelstroms, sheets of feedback ring like hammer blows, and three-chord vamps stretch to seven or eight minutes. Side D smears three distinct tracks into a 14-minute suite. (They even called one song “The Sprawl.”)

But *Daydream Nation* isn’t just a blueprint for the quiet-loud angst of Mogwai and Godspeed You! Black Emperor and decades of post-rock. “Silver Rocket” and “Hey Joni” are practically pop songs, just rewired for junkyard percussion and amps on fire, and they sit alongside head-scratchers like “Providence,” a three-way collision of upright piano, answering-machine message, and needle fluff. Sonic Youth would later get poppier (*Dirty*) and more experimental (*Goodbye 20th Century*), sometimes both at once (*Washing Machine*). But *Daydream Nation* is where they revealed just how wide indie rock’s horizons could be. –Philip Sherburne

