## Liz Phair: Exile in Guyville

![The 100 Best Indie Rock Albums of All Time](https://media.pitchfork.com/photos/6aee2f96ac3c503f2a26a6db/master/w_1600,c_limit/Liz%20Phair%20%E2%80%94%20Exile%20in%20Guyville.jpg)

The 100 Best Indie Rock Albums of All Time

![Image may contain Accessories Jewelry Necklace Adult Person Wedding Publication Face Head and Book](https://media.pitchfork.com/photos/6aee2be6d19586adfe8e354b/master/w_1600,c_limit/Liz%20Phair%20%E2%80%93%20Exile%20in%20Guyville.jpg)

Image may contain Accessories Jewelry Necklace Adult Person Wedding Publication Face Head and Book

When discussing Liz Phair’s epochal debut, it’s important to acknowledge the gender politics of rock and the particular challenges of relationships as a young woman in a milieu dominated by men—in the early ’90s, or at any time since. The record wouldn’t have resonated so strongly if Phair hadn’t been singing about these subjects with wit, insight, and miles of style for an audience that’d been starving for it. But *Exile in Guyville* is equally significant as an astonishing example of a once-in-a-lifetime outpouring of creative energy, one incubated in solitude and unleashed upon an unsuspecting scene that’s been reckoning with it ever since.

Half of the album’s 18 tracks were initially written and recorded by Phair when she was alone in her childhood bedroom, messing around with a guitar, microphone, and four-track machine. As the diaristic songs piled up, she bounced them to tapes and cobbled together artwork, eventually sharing them with friends under the name Girly-Sound. Cassettes were passed around, important minds were blown, and she ultimately got a record deal. I imagine the strength of the work surprised even its creator, and she returned to the cache of material for years afterward. Though born of anger and frustration, Phair’s musings on love and sex and stupid old shit like letters and sodas now sound like freedom, the product of an artist first realizing the full power of her gift. –Mark Richardson

