## Pavement: Crooked Rain, Crooked Rain

![Image may contain Accessories Jewelry Gemstone and Emerald](https://media.pitchfork.com/photos/6aee2f9678d0dfa19820ef69/master/w_1600,c_limit/Pavement%20%E2%80%94%20Crooked%20Rain%20Crooked%20Rain.jpg)

Image may contain Accessories Jewelry Gemstone and Emerald

Matador ✦ 1994

If history must be written by the winners, as the dictum goes, then we should be humble when conceiving a canon of artists who never seemed to place too much stock in winning. Just imagine Stephen Malkmus’ embarrassed, catlike reaction to being informed of his band’s exact placement on this list. So why *Crooked Rain, Crooked Rain*? As with all important matters, the argument is more existential than factual.

If it’s lazy to stick Pavement with the whole “slacker” thing—bands don’t make several records and play together in four separate decades without *trying* —we can still recognize, in their sound and style and general everything, a spirit of principled indecision. A suspicion of getting too settled. They were always changing their mind, often within the songs themselves, which toggle between earnest and elliptical, concise and jammy, discordant and catchy, clever and dour, withholding and confessional, aloof and focused, bitingly bitchy and heartbreakingly romantic. *Crooked Rain* is where those impulses and tensions crystallized in perfect formation, made before they got too self-conscious about their ““““fame”””” (as many ironic quotation marks as needed) or started hating each other. You really shouldn’t take Malkmus’ lyrics at face value, but sometimes he surprises us with what only feels like the truth: “Don’t worry, we’re in no hurry,” he wails on “Range Life,” a naked expression of purpose that nonetheless pops up on a song more infamous as a Smashing Pumpkins diss track.

The 1990s cannot be sloppily memorialized as the decade of the American layabout; this was also the time of the Clintonite yuppies, Web 1.0 techno-optimism, regional turf wars in hip-hop, teenaged pop stars. *Titanic* meant more to more people than Pavement. But it was probably the last decade where a fundamental divide between underground and overground was still perceivable. You knew where you stood, in relation to the monocultural center, and these guys were way off in the distance, squinting at the sun and getting lost. Everything was optional; rent was cheaper, too, as rent always is. Isn’t that something to be nostalgic for—the freedom to figure it out on your own time? To show up when you’re good and ready?

Indie rock is a genre whose emotions are often slippery, sly, just out of sight—the truth told slant, as Emily Dickinson put it in a line that became the title of Pavement’s first album. The music resists certainty; if you prefer Guided by Voices or Liz Phair, it would be very un-cool to insist you’re wrong. It’s easier to bring this back to Pitchfork itself, a website launched 30 years ago in the waning days of the underground’s brief cultural triumph, right before mainstream rock swung back to “mostly terrible.” Pitchfork’s founding mission was simple: something interesting is happening on the margins, so why not talk about it? Pavement is all-margin. Some albums start with a bang; in this one, we get the sound of a bunch of stuff falling over. Fittingly, they weren’t even that popular even when the weirdos were on top—but when indie rockers flop, it’s better that way. As Malkmus put it, “Cut Your Hair,” the record’s only “hit” (it peaked at No. 10 on *Billboard* ’s Alternative Airplay chart, right behind “Spoonman”), attention and fame’s a career. Take the weird and wrong path. –Jeremy Gordon

